Why console no gui menu based application not so used?

Because **menu-based console applications are usually a learning/testing style, 
not a common end-user product style**.

### Why they are less used

| Reason                                | Explanation                                                                                    |
| ------------------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| **Poor user experience**              | Most users prefer buttons, forms, windows, dashboards, etc.                                    |
| **Limited interaction**               | Console menus are mostly keyboard-driven and less intuitive.                                   |
| **No rich visualization**             | Difficult to show charts, maps, images, complex dashboards, drag/drop, etc.                    |
| **Platform limitations**              | Terminal behavior and UI differ across Windows/Linux/macOS.                                    |
| **Modern alternatives**               | GUI, web UI, mobile UI and REST APIs are more common for applications.                         |
| **Business products**                 | Customers generally expect a graphical or web interface.                                       |
| **Terminal is better for automation** | Professional command-line tools usually use commands/options rather than large numbered menus. |

For example, this:

```text
===== UAV Management =====

1. Connect
2. Arm
3. Takeoff
4. Mission
5. Telemetry
6. Land
7. Exit

Select:
```

is perfectly useful for **learning and prototyping**, but a real GCS would normally have a graphical interface.

### But console applications are still very important

The important distinction is:

**Menu-based console UI ≠ console/backend software.**

A lot of professional software has **no GUI at all**:

```text
          Application
               |
       +-------+-------+
       |               |
   Core Logic       Services
       |               |
       +-------+-------+
               |
        CLI / REST / GUI
```

For example:

* Linux utilities
* server applications
* backend services
* database tools
* DevOps tools
* network diagnostic tools
* cloud infrastructure
* compilers
* build systems
* automation programs
* HFT/infrastructure software
* UAV SITL/testing tools

These can be entirely command-line based.

### For your C++ learning

Your idea of making a **menu-based C++ application first** is actually useful if you treat the menu as only a **UI layer**:

```text
                C++ Application
                       |
              +--------+--------+
              |                 |
          Core/Domain        Controller
              |                 |
              +--------+--------+
                       |
                 Console Menu
```

Later:

```text
                    Core
                     |
       +-------------+-------------+
       |             |             |
    Console         GUI           REST API
```

So your C++ business/domain logic doesn't need to be rewritten.

**The console menu itself isn't the valuable part. The reusable C++ logic underneath it is.**

For learning C++/DSA and building non-embedded applications, 
I would use **small menu-based console apps as practice**, 
but avoid spending too much time making the terminal UI sophisticated.
